  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDeep upper troughing lingers across the Northeast through the \nweekend while expansive surface high pressure gradually slides east \nout of the Plains. The resultant setup for us locally will favor \nperiodic lake effect rain and snow showers, most prevalent in the \ndaytime hours, supported by rounds of vorticity lobes streaming \nthrough and lake to air temperature delta-T exceeding favorable \ndifference. The primary uncertainty is where exactly any \nheavier showers or localized bands set up. Ensemble clusters \nexhibit little disagreement in the overall pattern which should \nsuggest that low-level flow holds on to a more northerly \ndirection on Friday before backing northwesterly into the \nweekend which should shift higher coverage of showers from\nEastern OH on Friday into Western PA for the weekend.\n\nFirst, for the lower elevation, REFS probability hones in an area of \n(initially) wet snow showers falling into marginal surface \ntemperatures in the pre-dawn hours of Friday morning from Erie \nCounty down into Mercer County and pivoting east through the \nmorning. This should mostly turn over to rain as temperatures warm \ninto the upper 30s to low 40s, though evaporative cooling with\nprecipitation falling into a surface with near-freezing dew \npoints could continue to mix in some wet snowflakes with the \nrain in any more persistent, stronger bands. Could envision \nperhaps some white patches on the grass and elevated surfaces, \nbut no notable accumulation. Elsewhere, expect diurnally-driven\ncoverage of scattered rain showers each day through the weekend\nwith modest daytime heating steepening low-level lapse rates. \nSome snowflakes could mix in as far south as Pittsburgh metro \nearly Saturday morning with no accumulation expected. Overall \ncoverage is favored to be greater for Eastern OH on Friday and \nfor Western PA on Saturday and Sunday as low-level flow backs\nmore northwesterly and directs coverage farther east. All told,\nprobability for >0.5\" of snow reaches about 50-60% along I-80, \nthough think that will be periodic accumulation in the periods \nfavoring colder temperatures (overnight and early morning) with \nmelting in between.\n\nWhere it likely does remain cold enough for snow to be more\nfavored will be the PA and WV ridges, though the most favorable\nlow-level flow direction for upslope snow won't come until \nSaturday. Some light snow showers are possible on Friday, but \ncoverage should be at its most on Saturday. Even with that, \nmoisture depth remains trapped relatively shallow beneath a \nsinking subsidence inversion barely allowing the depth to reach \nthe DGZ at times and resulting in less favorable snow growth. \nProbability for >1\" of snow focuses on the higher elevation \nPreston and Tucker at 45-55%. Overall limited liquid-equivalent\nand lack of deep DGZ moisture looks to lend a low chance of\nexceeding much more than that (10-20% of >2\").\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nUpper troughing exits on Monday as the surface high eventually \nbecomes the dominant feature. Increasing subsidence and flow backing \nmore westerly will cut off the moisture fetch and mixing will \ngradually erode lingering stratocu coverage. Dry weather returns \nacross the board into at least the middle of next week with \ntemperatures eventually moderating back to above-normal values as \nthe airmass modifies in weak southerly flow.",
